# Line endings.
#
# Development happens on Windows, CI runs on Linux, and two kinds of file break if they carry
# CRLF into a Linux container:
#   *.sh  — a shebang line ending in CR makes the kernel look for an interpreter named
#           "/bin/bash\r", which fails with a bare "no such file or directory".
#   *.sql — sqlcmd tolerates CRLF, but a stray CR inside a quoted string or a sqlcmd variable
#           travels silently into the database, so the safe form is the same everywhere.
#
# These are forced to LF in the working tree as well as in the repository, so what a Windows
# developer runs through Docker is byte-identical to what CI runs.

* text=auto

*.sh   text eol=lf
*.sql  text eol=lf

# Same reasoning: these are read by Linux tooling or by Docker.
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.dockerfile    text eol=lf
docker-compose*.yml text eol=lf
.github/**/*.yml    text eol=lf
.github/**/*.yaml   text eol=lf

# Binary, never touched.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
